-- title:
--   The cone gap bounds the radius of an inscribed ball
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a real inner product space, $v \in E$ a unit vector, and $\vartheta$ an angle with $0 < \vartheta \le \pi/2$. For $p \in E$ let
--   $$g_{v,\vartheta}(p) \;=\; \langle v,p\rangle \sin\vartheta \;-\; \bigl\| p - \langle v,p\rangle v \bigr\| \cos\vartheta$$
--   be the cone gap of $p$, and for a set $S$ and a radius $\rho$ let
--   $$S_\rho \;=\; \{\, y \in S \;:\; \bar B(y,\rho) \subseteq S \,\}$$
--   be the *shrinking* of $S$ by $\rho$ (`shrink S ρ`), the set of points of $S$ that carry a closed ball of radius $\rho$ still inside $S$. The claim is: if $\rho \ge 0$ and
--   $$\rho < g_{v,\vartheta}(p),$$
--   then $p \in \bigl(\mathcal{C}(v,\vartheta)\bigr)_\rho$; that is, the closed ball of radius $\rho$ about $p$ is entirely contained in the cone $\mathcal{C}(v,\vartheta)$ with axis $v$ and half-angle $\vartheta$.
--
--   Applied to a double cone, the shrinking operation produces the *double half-cone* of the underlying theory, so this lemma is the quantitative statement that the cone gap really is a lower bound for the distance from $p$ to the complement of the cone. It is the tool that upgrades a single point known to lie in a cone into a whole ball of such points, which is what any argument needing a set of positive measure of intermediate points must have.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The inequality $\rho < g_{v,\vartheta}(p)$ is strict while the ball is closed, and $\rho = 0$ is permitted, in which case the conclusion reduces to $p \in \mathcal{C}(v,\vartheta)$.
